From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JR Lomas <lomas.jr@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 12:00:11 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] serialhdl: Validate CAN node ids before encoding

connect_canbus() accepted arbitrary integers even though the administrative message reserves one byte for the node id and the derived standard CAN identifiers assume that range.  This was reachable through console.py as well as internal callers and otherwise failed later in python-can with a transport-specific exception.

Reject values outside the protocol range before constructing either the arbitration identifier or assignment payload, producing the normal Klipper connection error instead.

Signed-off-by: JR Lomas <lomas.jr@gmail.com>

diff --git a/klippy/serialhdl.py b/klippy/serialhdl.py
index a25ce34d..bab6eb89 100644
--- a/klippy/serialhdl.py
+++ b/klippy/serialhdl.py
@@ -118,6 +118,15 @@ class SerialReader:
         return True
     def connect_canbus(self, canbus_uuid, canbus_nodeid, canbus_iface="can0"):
         import can # XXX
+        # Most callers obtain a checked id from canbus_ids, but console and
+        # diagnostic users can reach this transport entry point directly.  The
+        # assignment command has only one payload byte for the node id, and the
+        # corresponding standard CAN ids are calculated from the same range.
+        # Validate before either representation is built so an invalid caller
+        # receives a normal Klipper connection error instead of an incidental
+        # python-can packing or arbitration-id exception.
+        if canbus_nodeid < 0 or canbus_nodeid > 0xff:
+            self._error("Invalid CAN nodeid")
         txid = canbus_nodeid * 2 + 256
         filters = [{"can_id": txid+1, "can_mask": 0x7ff, "extended": False}]
         # Prep for SET_NODEID command
